-- The address of a local handed to a callee is exposure *for the call*, not for the frame.
--
-- Contain.Call.yana is this one level up: a record passed whole to a callee stays contained,
-- because an unretained argument is reachable only while the callee runs. `computeContainment`
-- refused the same statement about a `Borrow` - a local any borrow named was exposed for the rest
-- of the frame - and that is the form ordinary container code takes. `push(xs, 0)` is a borrow of a
-- local, so a loop that both writes through a container's elements and reads its header reloaded
-- the header every iteration, having no way to know that nothing in the loop could name it.
--
-- The rule is the same one: every reader of the borrow has to be a call the summary says retains
-- nothing, and then no address of the local exists anywhere outside those calls. A borrow used as a
-- place *root* is refused, since a read or a write through one is an access to the local's own
-- storage and the aliasing rules answer such a place by saying it cannot meet a local-rooted one.
--
-- Both halves are asserted here, because both failures are silent in the IR alone.

data Flags {read: Bool, write: Bool}

-- Writes through the borrow, and retains nothing: the address does not outlive the call.
@noinline
fn mutate(f: &Flags) -> Int:
    f.read = True
    return 0

-- The fold above the call and the reload below it, in one function.
--
-- `f.write` is read before anything has had the address at all, so the construction answers it.
-- `f.read` is read after a callee held the address for the length of one instruction, so it is a
-- load - and the fact that it is the *other* field is deliberately not used: what a callee did with
-- an address is not something a summary records, so the whole local is forgotten at the call.
fn around() -> Int:
    let &f = Flags {read: False, write: True}

    let before = if f.write then 10 else 20
    let handed = mutate(f)
    let after = if f.read then 100 else 200

    return before + handed + after

{-
   The shape §11.1 of test/bench/findings.md is about.

   The first loop borrows `xs` once per iteration for `push`, so nothing there is invariant. The
   second borrows nothing: it writes through the container's *elements*, which are the heap buffer
   rather than the local, and reads the container's header. Both header words - the length the
   bounds check reads and the buffer address the subscript adds to - leave the body, because the
   local they are rooted in is one no pointer in the function can be pointing at.

   The number is what says the writes still happened: `[0, 1, 2]` each raised by one is 1+2+3.
-}
fn fill(n: Int) -> Int:
    let &xs = [] :: [Int]
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < n:
        push(xs, i)
        i = i + 1

    let &sum = 0 :: Int
    let &k = 0 :: Int

    while k < n:
        xs[k] = xs[k] + 1
        sum = sum + xs[k]
        k = k + 1

    return sum

fn main() -> Int:
    return around() + fill(3)
